, image-alt markup, or an AST supplied directly to a renderer. It also does not rewrite generated heading and footnote anchors. Keep callbacks deterministic and free of side effects; parsing a nested link can inspect a destination that does not become a rendered link. Enabling raw HTML is not an image-policy control."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"raw-html","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raw HTML"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowHtml: true"},{"type":"text","value":" is an explicit trusted-content boundary:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"renderHtml(source, { allowHtml: true })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The HTML renderer emits raw nodes directly. The React and Octane renderers use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dangerouslySetInnerHTML"},{"type":"text","value":". Do not enable this option for untrusted user content unless the result is sanitized with a policy appropriate for your application."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"syntax-highlighters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Syntax highlighters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"highlighter"},{"type":"text","value":" returns HTML that is inserted into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<code>"},{"type":"text","value":" without further escaping:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"renderHtml(source, { highlighter })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only use a highlighter that escapes source code and returns trusted markup. TanStack Markdown cannot distinguish token markup from an injection in the returned string."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The tested "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/syntax-highlighting.md#tanstack-highlight-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Highlight adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" returns escaped inner token markup without duplicating Markdown's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<pre><code>"},{"type":"text","value":" containers."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"extensions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extensions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An extension "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderHtml"},{"type":"text","value":" hook also returns trusted HTML. React and Octane component replacements can enforce application-specific link, image, and navigation policies, but those replacements are outside core renderer parity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"document-asts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Document ASTs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderers trust document ASTs supplied directly by the application. URL screening happens during Markdown parsing, not when rendering an arbitrary link or image node. Do not accept untrusted JSON as a document AST without validating its structure and applying your URL and HTML policies."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An application-validated image node can contain a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data:"},{"type":"text","value":" URL and render through HTML, React, or Octane without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowHtml"},{"type":"text","value":". A custom parser can supply a validated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkdownDocument"},{"type":"text","value":" directly. For Markdown strings, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"urlTransform"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of trying to recover removed URLs in an inline transform."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resource-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resource limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core limits parser nesting and inline delimiter scans. These are not a limit on total input size, footnote count, or work performed by extensions. Bound untrusted document sizes in the application, and batch streaming updates instead of rerendering on every incoming character."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"untrusted-content","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Untrusted content"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For user-generated Markdown:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowHtml"},{"type":"text","value":" disabled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not install extensions that emit unsanitized HTML."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Audit or sanitize highlighter output."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply application policies for outbound links and remote images."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sanitize the final HTML when your threat model requires an independent defense layer."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The security regression suite covers executable protocols, raw HTML opt-in behavior, escaping, and framework renderer handling."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"security","text":"Security","level":1},{"id":"default-policy","text":"Default policy","level":2},{"id":"custom-url-policy","text":"Custom URL policy","level":2},{"id":"raw-html","text":"Raw HTML","level":2},{"id":"syntax-highlighters","text":"Syntax highlighters","level":2},{"id":"extensions","text":"Extensions","level":2},{"id":"document-asts","text":"Document ASTs","level":2},{"id":"resource-limits","text":"Resource limits","level":2},{"id":"untrusted-content","text":"Untrusted content","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Security\n\nTanStack Markdown has safe defaults, but it is not a general HTML sanitizer.\n\n## Default policy\n\nBy default:\n\n- raw block and inline HTML is escaped\n- `javascript:`, `vbscript:`, `file:`, and dangerous `data:` URLs are removed\n- text, attributes, code, link titles, and image alt text are escaped by the HTML renderer\n- React and Octane render ordinary values rather than injecting HTML\n\nRelative URLs, fragments, HTTP, HTTPS, email, and telephone links remain available. Other explicit protocols, including `data:`, are removed.\n\n## Custom URL policy\n\n`urlTransform(url, kind, defaultUrl)` runs while parsing Markdown link and image destinations, including reference destinations. It receives the parsed URL before screening, `'link'` or `'image'`, and the result of the built-in URL policy. Return `defaultUrl` to keep that policy, a nonempty string to replace it, or `null` to remove the link or image while keeping its label content. An empty string keeps the existing empty-URL behavior: images have an empty `src`, nonempty link destinations lose their wrapper, and empty source destinations remain empty. Prefer `null` when rejecting a URL.\n\nFor example, an application can allow only images it has already validated:\n\n```ts\nimport { renderHtml } from '@tanstack/markdown'\nimport type { UrlTransform } from '@tanstack/markdown'\n\nfunction imagePolicy(approvedImages: ReadonlySet<string>): UrlTransform {\n  return (url, kind, defaultUrl) =>\n    kind === 'image' && approvedImages.has(url) ? url : defaultUrl\n}\n\nconst html = renderHtml('![Logo](data:image/png;base64,...)', {\n  urlTransform: imagePolicy(new Set()),\n})\n```\n\nPopulate the set only after validating image content, MIME type, and payload size. Do not approve every `data:` URL or populate it from untrusted Markdown. Callback results are trusted and are not screened again, though renderers still escape attribute values. Returning `defaultUrl` for links preserves the built-in protocol restrictions.\n\nThe callback is synchronous and runs before inline transforms. It does not screen raw HTML, extension-created URLs, image-alt markup, or an AST supplied directly to a renderer. It also does not rewrite generated heading and footnote anchors. Keep callbacks deterministic and free of side effects; parsing a nested link can inspect a destination that does not become a rendered link. Enabling raw HTML is not an image-policy control.\n\n## Raw HTML\n\n`allowHtml: true` is an explicit trusted-content boundary:\n\n```ts\nrenderHtml(source, { allowHtml: true })\n```\n\nThe HTML renderer emits raw nodes directly. The React and Octane renderers use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Do not enable this option for untrusted user content unless the result is sanitized with a policy appropriate for your application.\n\n## Syntax highlighters\n\nA `highlighter` returns HTML that is inserted into `<code>` without further escaping:\n\n```ts\nrenderHtml(source, { highlighter })\n```\n\nOnly use a highlighter that escapes source code and returns trusted markup. TanStack Markdown cannot distinguish token markup from an injection in the returned string.\n\nThe tested [TanStack Highlight adapter](../guides/syntax-highlighting.md#tanstack-highlight-adapter) returns escaped inner token markup without duplicating Markdown's `<pre><code>` containers.\n\n## Extensions\n\nAn extension `renderHtml` hook also returns trusted HTML. React and Octane component replacements can enforce application-specific link, image, and navigation policies, but those replacements are outside core renderer parity.\n\n## Document ASTs\n\nRenderers trust document ASTs supplied directly by the application. URL screening happens during Markdown parsing, not when rendering an arbitrary link or image node. Do not accept untrusted JSON as a document AST without validating its structure and applying your URL and HTML policies.\n\nAn application-validated image node can contain a `data:` URL and render through HTML, React, or Octane without `allowHtml`. A custom parser can supply a validated `MarkdownDocument` directly. For Markdown strings, use `urlTransform` instead of trying to recover removed URLs in an inline transform.\n\n## Resource limits\n\nThe core limits parser nesting and inline delimiter scans. These are not a limit on total input size, footnote count, or work performed by extensions. Bound untrusted document sizes in the application, and batch streaming updates instead of rerendering on every incoming character.\n\n## Untrusted content\n\nFor user-generated Markdown:\n\n1. Keep `allowHtml` disabled.\n2. Do not install extensions that emit unsanitized HTML.\n3. Audit or sanitize highlighter output.\n4. Apply application policies for outbound links and remote images.\n5. Sanitize the final HTML when your threat model requires an independent defense layer.\n\nThe security regression suite covers executable protocols, raw HTML opt-in behavior, escaping, and framework renderer handling.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Security\n---\n\n# Security\n\nTanStack Markdown has safe defaults, but it is not a general HTML sanitizer.\n\n## Default policy\n\nBy default:\n\n- raw block and inline HTML is escaped\n- `javascript:`, `vbscript:`, `file:`, and dangerous `data:` URLs are removed\n- text, attributes, code, link titles, and image alt text are escaped by the HTML renderer\n- React and Octane render ordinary values rather than injecting HTML\n\nRelative URLs, fragments, HTTP, HTTPS, email, and telephone links remain available. Other explicit protocols, including `data:`, are removed.\n\n## Custom URL policy\n\n`urlTransform(url, kind, defaultUrl)` runs while parsing Markdown link and image destinations, including reference destinations. It receives the parsed URL before screening, `'link'` or `'image'`, and the result of the built-in URL policy. Return `defaultUrl` to keep that policy, a nonempty string to replace it, or `null` to remove the link or image while keeping its label content. An empty string keeps the existing empty-URL behavior: images have an empty `src`, nonempty link destinations lose their wrapper, and empty source destinations remain empty. Prefer `null` when rejecting a URL.\n\nFor example, an application can allow only images it has already validated:\n\n```ts\nimport { renderHtml } from '@tanstack/markdown'\nimport type { UrlTransform } from '@tanstack/markdown'\n\nfunction imagePolicy(approvedImages: ReadonlySet<string>): UrlTransform {\n  return (url, kind, defaultUrl) =>\n    kind === 'image' && approvedImages.has(url) ? url : defaultUrl\n}\n\nconst html = renderHtml('![Logo](data:image/png;base64,...)', {\n  urlTransform: imagePolicy(new Set()),\n})\n```\n\nPopulate the set only after validating image content, MIME type, and payload size. Do not approve every `data:` URL or populate it from untrusted Markdown. Callback results are trusted and are not screened again, though renderers still escape attribute values. Returning `defaultUrl` for links preserves the built-in protocol restrictions.\n\nThe callback is synchronous and runs before inline transforms. It does not screen raw HTML, extension-created URLs, image-alt markup, or an AST supplied directly to a renderer. It also does not rewrite generated heading and footnote anchors. Keep callbacks deterministic and free of side effects; parsing a nested link can inspect a destination that does not become a rendered link. Enabling raw HTML is not an image-policy control.\n\n## Raw HTML\n\n`allowHtml: true` is an explicit trusted-content boundary:\n\n```ts\nrenderHtml(source, { allowHtml: true })\n```\n\nThe HTML renderer emits raw nodes directly. The React and Octane renderers use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Do not enable this option for untrusted user content unless the result is sanitized with a policy appropriate for your application.\n\n## Syntax highlighters\n\nA `highlighter` returns HTML that is inserted into `<code>` without further escaping:\n\n```ts\nrenderHtml(source, { highlighter })\n```\n\nOnly use a highlighter that escapes source code and returns trusted markup. TanStack Markdown cannot distinguish token markup from an injection in the returned string.\n\nThe tested [TanStack Highlight adapter](../guides/syntax-highlighting.md#tanstack-highlight-adapter) returns escaped inner token markup without duplicating Markdown's `<pre><code>` containers.\n\n## Extensions\n\nAn extension `renderHtml` hook also returns trusted HTML. React and Octane component replacements can enforce application-specific link, image, and navigation policies, but those replacements are outside core renderer parity.\n\n## Document ASTs\n\nRenderers trust document ASTs supplied directly by the application. URL screening happens during Markdown parsing, not when rendering an arbitrary link or image node. Do not accept untrusted JSON as a document AST without validating its structure and applying your URL and HTML policies.\n\nAn application-validated image node can contain a `data:` URL and render through HTML, React, or Octane without `allowHtml`. A custom parser can supply a validated `MarkdownDocument` directly. For Markdown strings, use `urlTransform` instead of trying to recover removed URLs in an inline transform.\n\n## Resource limits\n\nThe core limits parser nesting and inline delimiter scans. These are not a limit on total input size, footnote count, or work performed by extensions. Bound untrusted document sizes in the application, and batch streaming updates instead of rerendering on every incoming character.\n\n## Untrusted content\n\nFor user-generated Markdown:\n\n1. Keep `allowHtml` disabled.\n2. Do not install extensions that emit unsanitized HTML.\n3. Audit or sanitize highlighter output.\n4. Apply application policies for outbound links and remote images.\n5. Sanitize the final HTML when your threat model requires an independent defense layer.\n\nThe security regression suite covers executable protocols, raw HTML opt-in behavior, escaping, and framework renderer handling.\n"}
